    "title": "More Countries Move to Limit Teen Social Media Use",
    "chineseTitle": "更多國家著手限制青少年使用社群媒體",
    "passage": "Growing concern over children's online safety has led more countries to limit their access to social media. Australia became the first country to prohibit children under 16 from having social media accounts, and other governments have followed suit by announcing similar plans. The European Union is preparing new age-verification rules, while the UK has proposed a default overnight curfew to stop teenagers from using social media between midnight and 6 a.m.\n\nEarly results from Australia, however, suggest that putting such policies into practice may prove more difficult than expected. Reports indicate that some children there have still managed to create social media accounts despite the new restrictions, raising questions about whether current age-verification methods are strong enough. In response, regulators have increased pressure on social media companies to strengthen their verification systems.\n\nThese developments are now at the center of a worldwide debate. Supporters of the new rules say they could reduce cyberbullying, improve sleep, and protect children from harmful content. Critics argue they may also limit access to helpful information and support communities. Most experts agree that children need stronger online protection. However, many believe safety tools, parental involvement, and digital literacy education should be part of the solution, not age limits alone.",
    "chineseTranslation": "對兒童網路安全的擔憂日益增加，促使更多國家限制兒童使用社群媒體。澳洲成為第一個禁止16歲以下兒童擁有社群媒體帳號的國家，其他政府也紛紛跟進，宣布類似的計畫。歐盟正在研擬新的年齡驗證規定，英國則提議實施預設的夜間宵禁，禁止青少年在午夜至清晨6點之間使用社群媒體。\n\n然而，澳洲的初步結果顯示，要落實這類政策可能比預期中困難。報導指出，儘管有新的限制，當地仍有些兒童成功建立了社群媒體帳號，令人質疑現行的年齡驗證方法是否夠嚴謹。為此，監管機關加強向社群媒體公司施壓，要求它們強化驗證系統。\n\n這些發展如今成為全球辯論的焦點。新規定的支持者表示，這些規定能減少網路霸凌、改善睡眠，並保護兒童免於接觸有害內容。批評者則認為，這些規定也可能限制兒童取得有用的資訊和支持社群。大多數專家都同意兒童需要更強的網路保護，但許多人認為，安全工具、家長參與以及數位素養教育都應該是解決方案的一部分，而不能只靠年齡限制。",
